1
00:00:03,983 --> 00:00:06,425
[SPEAKER_02]: Let me just practice in there, see how it's done.

2
00:00:06,885 --> 00:00:14,692
[SPEAKER_02]: We're at Mr. Joe McCarthy's home in Tonawanda, New York, on Tuesday, April 15, 1975.

3
00:00:14,872 --> 00:00:15,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's see.

4
00:00:15,652 --> 00:00:18,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Right now, right where we are.

5
00:00:19,055 --> 00:00:22,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Pick up OK. Well, this is all right, Mr. Murdock.

6
00:00:22,078 --> 00:00:29,644
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm very happy to be here with you, and I hope I give you some answers that you're looking for.

7
00:00:29,684 --> 00:00:30,544
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I'm sure you will.

8
00:00:30,584 --> 00:00:32,266
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a real thrill for me to be here.

9
00:00:32,566 --> 00:00:33,687
[SPEAKER_02]: Remember that picture there?

10
00:00:36,285 --> 00:00:36,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

11
00:00:37,706 --> 00:00:41,167
[SPEAKER_01]: That was with the Cubs.

12
00:00:41,187 --> 00:00:42,808
[SPEAKER_01]: That was taken out in Catalina Island.

13
00:00:42,828 --> 00:00:44,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you know where I got that from?

14
00:00:44,268 --> 00:00:45,189
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I couldn't tell you.

15
00:00:45,209 --> 00:00:45,769
[SPEAKER_02]: Fred Blake.

16
00:00:46,429 --> 00:00:46,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Where?

17
00:00:46,870 --> 00:00:47,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Fred Blake.

18
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:48,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Fred Blake.

19
00:00:48,830 --> 00:00:49,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, Sheriff Blake.

20
00:00:49,911 --> 00:00:51,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, down in Bluefield.

21
00:00:52,092 --> 00:00:52,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, Virginia.

22
00:00:53,272 --> 00:00:54,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Or West Virginia.

23
00:00:54,193 --> 00:00:55,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Bluefield, West Virginia.

24
00:00:55,313 --> 00:00:55,813
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

25
00:00:55,833 --> 00:00:59,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he was a great pitcher and a great friend of mine.

26
00:01:00,155 --> 00:01:01,156
[SPEAKER_01]: We called him Sheriff.

27
00:01:01,176 --> 00:01:01,956
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, Sheriff.

28
00:01:02,386 --> 00:01:04,087
[SPEAKER_02]: We had a real good interview with him.

29
00:01:04,427 --> 00:01:06,548
[SPEAKER_02]: He's real nice.

30
00:01:06,768 --> 00:01:10,829
[SPEAKER_02]: You got a good story to tell?

31
00:01:12,390 --> 00:01:13,890
[SPEAKER_02]: Got some stories to tell about him?

32
00:01:15,331 --> 00:01:18,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, they're all good.

33
00:01:18,232 --> 00:01:28,256
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember when I left Chicago, he came up to the hotel the night I was leaving and told me he was very sorry.

34
00:01:29,806 --> 00:01:33,367
[SPEAKER_01]: A few players that did that and he's very sorry to see me go.

35
00:01:34,827 --> 00:01:38,508
[SPEAKER_01]: And I surely appreciated it.

36
00:01:39,028 --> 00:01:42,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you come back?

37
00:01:42,549 --> 00:01:44,009
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm watching a pheasant out there.

38
00:01:45,229 --> 00:01:46,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you see that cottage?

39
00:01:46,589 --> 00:01:47,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's a little dollhouse.

40
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:48,430
[SPEAKER_01]: I just love it.

41
00:02:00,643 --> 00:02:07,166
[SPEAKER_02]: You were born in Philadelphia in 1887.

42
00:02:07,686 --> 00:02:12,328
[SPEAKER_01]: What did your father do?

43
00:02:12,368 --> 00:02:14,989
[SPEAKER_01]: My father was a contractor.

44
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:17,610
[SPEAKER_01]: My father was killed

45
00:02:22,111 --> 00:02:27,774
[SPEAKER_01]: He had a job doing the work on the Mannheim cricket grounds.

46
00:02:27,794 --> 00:02:30,376
[SPEAKER_01]: That was a great place where they played cricket years ago.

47
00:02:30,836 --> 00:02:31,717
[SPEAKER_01]: He was killed there.

48
00:02:31,737 --> 00:02:40,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, I was only three months old at the time and my mother had to just take charge and raise me the best she could.

49
00:02:41,002 --> 00:02:42,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Were there other children in the family?

50
00:02:42,623 --> 00:02:45,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there was a few, yes.

51
00:02:45,285 --> 00:02:46,045
[SPEAKER_01]: I won't go into that.

52
00:02:46,966 --> 00:02:48,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Not too many children.

53
00:02:50,303 --> 00:02:52,866
[SPEAKER_02]: That was quite a feat for her to raise.

54
00:02:53,327 --> 00:02:54,789
[SPEAKER_02]: How'd she do it?

55
00:02:56,411 --> 00:03:01,638
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of things were done years ago that you probably wouldn't want to do today and you wouldn't have to do today.

56
00:03:02,439 --> 00:03:04,581
[SPEAKER_01]: You go out and work, that's the only thing.

57
00:03:05,543 --> 00:03:07,886
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess that's the answer to everything.

58
00:03:08,726 --> 00:03:11,789
[SPEAKER_01]: what made our country and everything was real hard work.

59
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:14,251
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you live in the city?

60
00:03:15,151 --> 00:03:17,333
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we lived out in Germantown.

61
00:03:17,934 --> 00:03:19,575
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the 22nd Ward.

62
00:03:19,635 --> 00:03:25,580
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a separate little village years ago, then it was taken in by the city.

63
00:03:26,361 --> 00:03:29,924
[SPEAKER_02]: Had your parents lived in this country very long or did they come over?

64
00:03:29,964 --> 00:03:33,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, my mother was born in Ireland and my father was born in Ireland.

65
00:03:33,326 --> 00:03:34,588
[SPEAKER_02]: Were they married in Ireland?

66
00:03:34,648 --> 00:03:35,969
[SPEAKER_01]: No, they were married over here.

67
00:03:39,738 --> 00:03:41,219
[SPEAKER_02]: When did they come over from Ireland?

68
00:03:41,239 --> 00:03:44,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I couldn't tell you just to give you the date.

69
00:03:46,682 --> 00:03:57,949
[SPEAKER_01]: I know my mother came from the Ulster province in Ireland and my father was from down in the lower part of Ireland.

70
00:03:58,009 --> 00:03:58,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Was your mother Catholic?

71
00:03:59,370 --> 00:04:01,231
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, my mother was Catholic.

72
00:04:02,272 --> 00:04:07,055
[SPEAKER_01]: She came from Strabane, that was in the County Mayo in the town of

73
00:04:08,264 --> 00:04:09,405
[SPEAKER_01]: in the Ulster province.

74
00:04:10,345 --> 00:04:11,445
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a flax country.

75
00:04:13,026 --> 00:04:13,766
[SPEAKER_01]: I couldn't tell you.

76
00:04:13,786 --> 00:04:15,927
[SPEAKER_01]: I know my father came from Cork.

77
00:04:16,347 --> 00:04:20,929
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think there was much athletic work going on at the time.

78
00:04:20,949 --> 00:04:23,510
[SPEAKER_02]: You didn't have many baseball clubs in those days and you didn't have any other

79
00:04:36,351 --> 00:04:40,834
[SPEAKER_01]: athletic sports, I guess.

80
00:04:40,974 --> 00:04:43,035
[SPEAKER_02]: Was Germantown a kind of Irish settlement?

81
00:04:43,395 --> 00:04:49,679
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it was founded by a German by the name of Francis Daniel Pastorius.

82
00:04:49,719 --> 00:04:56,483
[SPEAKER_01]: They were unveiling a big monument in Vernon Park in Germantown for him when the first war broke out.

83
00:04:56,904 --> 00:05:01,106
[SPEAKER_01]: They counted him being a German and they had to board it up until the war was over.

84
00:05:05,019 --> 00:05:11,044
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the big ward in the city of Philadelphia, Germantown.

85
00:05:11,264 --> 00:05:11,804
[SPEAKER_01]: 22nd Ward.

86
00:05:13,146 --> 00:05:15,147
[SPEAKER_02]: When did you first play ball and where?

87
00:05:15,487 --> 00:05:27,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I played with a, around, not semi-pro, amateur around and then our club in Germantown was called the Chew AA.

88
00:05:28,058 --> 00:05:31,620
[SPEAKER_01]: And that was named after the great Chew family.

89
00:05:31,660 --> 00:05:34,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And Washington made his headquarters in the Chew Mansion.

90
00:05:35,224 --> 00:05:38,166
[SPEAKER_01]: during the Revolutionary War in Germantown.

91
00:05:38,186 --> 00:05:40,867
[SPEAKER_01]: They had a battle of Germantown.

92
00:05:41,788 --> 00:05:44,870
[SPEAKER_01]: So we named our ball club after the Chu family.

93
00:05:44,950 --> 00:05:49,393
[SPEAKER_01]: It was called the Chu AA, and that was really my first start in baseball.

94
00:05:49,773 --> 00:05:51,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you go to school in Germantown?

95
00:05:51,234 --> 00:05:52,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I went to school in Germantown.

96
00:05:52,735 --> 00:05:53,775
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you finish up high school there?

97
00:05:53,835 --> 00:05:54,516
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I didn't.

98
00:05:54,576 --> 00:05:56,057
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't get into high school.

99
00:05:56,537 --> 00:05:58,678
[SPEAKER_01]: But I did go up to Niagara University.

100
00:05:58,758 --> 00:06:01,040
[SPEAKER_01]: I was up there in 1905 and 1906.

101
00:06:03,968 --> 00:06:06,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I started playing professional baseball.

102
00:06:07,429 --> 00:06:11,731
[SPEAKER_02]: How did you get over to Niagara from Philadelphia?

103
00:06:13,072 --> 00:06:15,152
[SPEAKER_02]: Did your family move?

104
00:06:18,494 --> 00:06:23,236
[SPEAKER_01]: I was a pretty fair ball player at the time and Niagara was looking for good ball players.

105
00:06:23,296 --> 00:06:24,617
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess that's the only reason.

106
00:06:24,657 --> 00:06:27,098
[SPEAKER_01]: They recruited you?

107
00:06:28,478 --> 00:06:29,419
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know what you said.

108
00:06:31,013 --> 00:06:41,924
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, it was through some friends of mine and of course the big college was in Germantown, it was Niagara University.

109
00:06:42,164 --> 00:06:44,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, Niagara was in Germantown?

110
00:06:44,907 --> 00:06:47,650
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, Niagara University up at Niagara Falls.

111
00:06:48,325 --> 00:06:52,286
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm saying that the mother house was the St. John's in Brooklyn.

112
00:06:52,406 --> 00:06:56,967
[SPEAKER_01]: But then we had another St. Vincent de Paul parish in Germantown.

113
00:06:57,608 --> 00:07:02,489
[SPEAKER_01]: And through knowing a lot of people and the connection, that's how I got to Niagara University.

114
00:07:03,109 --> 00:07:05,790
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you have any trouble getting in, not having finished high school?

115
00:07:05,910 --> 00:07:07,290
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I didn't.

116
00:07:07,911 --> 00:07:09,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you like it at Niagara?

117
00:07:09,091 --> 00:07:10,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I liked it very much.

118
00:07:10,551 --> 00:07:11,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you play ball there?

119
00:07:11,952 --> 00:07:13,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I played ball there.

120
00:07:13,392 --> 00:07:15,933
[SPEAKER_01]: I only played the one season at the end of

121
00:07:17,063 --> 00:07:19,184
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, I was only there about one season.

122
00:07:19,824 --> 00:07:27,746
[SPEAKER_01]: At the end of 1906, I went out and started to play professional baseball.

123
00:07:28,426 --> 00:07:31,707
[SPEAKER_02]: Were you working in Germantown during those years?

124
00:07:31,907 --> 00:07:34,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, when I was a kid, I worked in mills.

125
00:07:36,368 --> 00:07:39,668
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd done all kinds of work, naturally.

126
00:07:41,069 --> 00:07:43,589
[SPEAKER_02]: Helped out the family finances.

127
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:45,790
[SPEAKER_01]: The best they could, yeah.

128
00:07:46,947 --> 00:07:52,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, now, did some professional scouts see you play at Niagara?

129
00:07:52,670 --> 00:07:54,231
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that how you got your first contract?

130
00:07:54,451 --> 00:08:07,378
[SPEAKER_01]: No, they didn't have too many scouts around in those days and I came home and then I went down and finished the season and I went to...

131
00:08:07,498 --> 00:08:09,539
[SPEAKER_02]: There's Wilmington here as your first team.

132
00:08:10,360 --> 00:08:10,920
[SPEAKER_02]: Wilmington?

133
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:15,322
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that was in... Wilmington was my first job in 1907.

134
00:08:16,716 --> 00:08:17,757
[SPEAKER_01]: But this was in six.

135
00:08:17,817 --> 00:08:21,040
[SPEAKER_01]: I went down and I was in the, they called it the Peach League.

136
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:23,282
[SPEAKER_01]: It was down in Delaware, Dover, Delaware.

137
00:08:24,223 --> 00:08:30,108
[SPEAKER_01]: And then I went up from there up to Catasauqua and the Lehigh Valley League.

138
00:08:30,128 --> 00:08:32,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Those leagues, they were good little leagues.

139
00:08:32,210 --> 00:08:36,634
[SPEAKER_01]: It was good semi-pro ball, but it wasn't under protection.

140
00:08:36,995 --> 00:08:42,620
[SPEAKER_01]: And then in 1907, I went with the Wilmington Club and the Tri-State League.

141
00:08:45,796 --> 00:08:47,216
[SPEAKER_02]: How did you get on that team?

142
00:08:47,236 --> 00:08:51,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody saw you?

143
00:08:51,437 --> 00:08:53,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

144
00:08:55,058 --> 00:08:56,899
[SPEAKER_01]: They gave me my release.

145
00:08:56,999 --> 00:09:03,361
[SPEAKER_01]: I wasn't good enough for the Wilmington Club so I went out to Franklin, Pennsylvania in the Interstate League.

146
00:09:04,321 --> 00:09:05,341
[SPEAKER_02]: Had a good year there.

147
00:09:06,261 --> 00:09:08,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I'd done pretty well in Franklin.

148
00:09:08,742 --> 00:09:10,743
[SPEAKER_02]: What did your mother think of your playing ball?

149
00:09:10,803 --> 00:09:12,884
[SPEAKER_01]: She liked it alright.

150
00:09:13,804 --> 00:09:17,525
[SPEAKER_01]: She said she had seen it in Ireland before but I don't think she did.

151
00:09:17,565 --> 00:09:19,986
[SPEAKER_01]: It was called rounders years ago.

152
00:09:22,167 --> 00:09:24,908
[SPEAKER_02]: Did she come to see you play much?

153
00:09:25,368 --> 00:09:26,589
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

154
00:09:28,950 --> 00:09:32,051
[SPEAKER_02]: She was too busy working.

155
00:09:34,399 --> 00:09:36,902
[SPEAKER_02]: You had a pretty good year at Franklin.

156
00:09:37,122 --> 00:09:38,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Did Toledo buy you?

157
00:09:38,783 --> 00:09:41,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, Toledo bought me from Franklin.

158
00:09:41,406 --> 00:09:43,968
[SPEAKER_01]: But that league broke up that winter.

159
00:09:44,088 --> 00:09:50,234
[SPEAKER_01]: The Interstate League disbanded, yes.

160
00:09:51,455 --> 00:09:53,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Was it a pretty tough competition there?

161
00:09:53,217 --> 00:09:54,698
[SPEAKER_02]: What classification would it have been?

162
00:09:58,347 --> 00:10:01,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Toledo was an American association.

163
00:10:01,629 --> 00:10:04,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, those leagues were class A in those days.

164
00:10:05,431 --> 00:10:09,473
[SPEAKER_01]: And then as the population grew, they raised them to double A.

165
00:10:10,233 --> 00:10:12,675
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they got from double A, now they're triple A.

166
00:10:13,215 --> 00:10:15,016
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't have anything below it, practically.

167
00:10:15,096 --> 00:10:16,497
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

168
00:10:17,037 --> 00:10:19,719
[SPEAKER_02]: Was Toledo independently owned then?

169
00:10:20,259 --> 00:10:25,622
[SPEAKER_01]: No, Toledo was owned by Charlie Summers, who owned the Cleveland Club at the time.

170
00:10:27,247 --> 00:10:28,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever meet Summers?

171
00:10:28,508 --> 00:10:31,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I met him once.

172
00:10:31,109 --> 00:10:35,672
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a big coal operator.

173
00:10:35,792 --> 00:10:39,975
[SPEAKER_01]: He built a ballpark in Toledo called Swainfield.

174
00:10:40,775 --> 00:10:44,417
[SPEAKER_01]: That was the finest minor league park built in the country in those days.

175
00:10:44,617 --> 00:10:45,578
[SPEAKER_02]: How many would it seat?

176
00:10:47,605 --> 00:10:51,708
[SPEAKER_01]: It would seat about, including the beaches, about 18-20,000.

177
00:10:51,748 --> 00:10:55,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you get pretty big crowds sometimes there?

178
00:10:55,871 --> 00:10:59,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, very well, as long as you won.

179
00:11:00,914 --> 00:11:01,755
[SPEAKER_02]: Who was the manager?

180
00:11:02,836 --> 00:11:06,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Bill Armour, he was one of the great managers.

181
00:11:06,258 --> 00:11:08,520
[SPEAKER_01]: He's the fellow who brought up Tide Cobb.

182
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:11,782
[SPEAKER_01]: He was president and manager of the Toledo Club.

183
00:11:12,222 --> 00:11:13,823
[SPEAKER_02]: He managed Cleveland for a while, didn't he?

184
00:11:13,863 --> 00:11:16,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, he did, and he managed Detroit for a while.

185
00:11:17,214 --> 00:11:20,677
[SPEAKER_02]: Did Bresnahan live there?

186
00:11:21,177 --> 00:11:24,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Bresnahan bought the club in later years.

187
00:11:24,961 --> 00:11:27,583
[SPEAKER_01]: The Giants owned the club.

188
00:11:29,445 --> 00:11:31,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether he bought it from the Giants or not, I don't know.

189
00:11:33,768 --> 00:11:35,970
[SPEAKER_01]: But he was in there as a manager and president.

190
00:11:36,010 --> 00:11:38,152
[SPEAKER_01]: He owned the ball club.

191
00:11:38,172 --> 00:11:39,293
[SPEAKER_02]: How did you like it at Toledo?

192
00:11:39,634 --> 00:11:40,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Very good.

193
00:11:40,955 --> 00:11:41,415
[SPEAKER_01]: I liked it.

194
00:11:43,288 --> 00:11:44,849
[SPEAKER_01]: That's three years here.

195
00:11:44,969 --> 00:11:47,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Nineteen-eight, nine, ten, and part of eleven.

196
00:11:47,471 --> 00:11:49,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Part of eleven, yes.

197
00:11:50,333 --> 00:11:52,494
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you play second base all these years?

198
00:11:52,614 --> 00:11:54,935
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I played any place.

199
00:11:55,055 --> 00:11:57,957
[SPEAKER_01]: I was a utility man.

200
00:11:58,718 --> 00:12:00,419
[SPEAKER_02]: What position did you prefer?

201
00:12:01,199 --> 00:12:03,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it didn't make much difference to me, I guess.

202
00:12:03,921 --> 00:12:06,062
[SPEAKER_01]: I could play the infield or outfield.

203
00:12:06,082 --> 00:12:10,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Not too good, but fair enough to hang around and I guess that was...

204
00:12:11,777 --> 00:12:13,458
[SPEAKER_01]: All you cared to do in those days.

205
00:12:14,179 --> 00:12:16,741
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you went from Toledo to Indianapolis.

206
00:12:16,821 --> 00:12:18,922
[SPEAKER_02]: What was the situation there?

207
00:12:19,002 --> 00:12:20,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it was the same thing in Indianapolis.

208
